        The other 8 scripts' Usage/POD already matched; those tests
        pass immediately, serving as a safety net for any future
        drift rather than fixing an existing one.

1.30  2026-09-10
      - SECURITY HARDENING (TGT-162, found via scheduled hourly bug hunt
        JOB-003, second pass): D2TG::Telegram::_send_file's caption
        field (from cli/send.pl's --caption flag) was spliced into the
        raw hand-built multipart body with zero sanitization, unlike
        the adjacent filename field, which was hardened for TGT-125
        (control-character stripping plus quote/backslash escaping)
        after a Codex review found the original escaping alone
        insufficient against header injection. The same trust boundary
        (a user-supplied cli/send.pl argument) got defense-in-depth on
        one field and none on the other. Caption sits in body content
        rather than a quoted header attribute, so its own real risk is
        narrower: if the caption text happens to embed the literal
        per-call multipart boundary string ('D2TGBoundary' .
        int(rand(1e9)) . time, regenerated and not attacker-visible
        each call) in real delimiter syntax (\r\n--$boundary with valid
        trailing framing, not the bare value alone), it could
        prematurely terminate the multipart body, letting trailing
        bytes be reinterpreted as new form fields (e.g. clobbering
        chat_id in the same request). _send_file now conservatively
        strips any occurrence of the literal boundary string from the
        caption - delimiter-shaped or not - before inserting it into
        the body. Deliberately does
        not add filename's own quote/backslash escaping or control-
        character stripping to caption - those exist to prevent header
        injection, which does not apply to plain body content where
        CR/LF is legitimate caption text. send_voice's own multipart
        construction is untouched (already reviewed, different code
        path).

1.29  2026-09-10
      - BUG FIX (TGT-161, found via scheduled hourly bug hunt JOB-003):
        D2TG::Poller::_media_kind only recognized photo/document/voice,
        so a video message (no plain text, no recognized media kind)
        failed run_once's own "next unless text or media_kind" guard
        and was silently dropped - not printed to stdout, not queued as
        NEW TG PENDING for an unapproved sender, not recorded in the
        store, no stderr line, and the poll offset still advanced past
        it. Gone forever, indistinguishable from an update that never
        arrived. _media_kind now also recognizes video
        ($message->{video}); the existing generic fallback branch (which
        video always reaches, since there is no video-specific handling
        or download path at all) already prints/records generically by
        media kind, so it needed no change to announce and record a
        video the same way an
        undownloaded photo/document already is. Actual video download
        support is a separate, larger decision and stays out of scope;
        video_note/audio/animation/sticker are the same failure class
        but are deliberately deferred to a follow-up ticket, to keep
        this fix narrow and reviewable.

1.28  2026-09-10
      - RELIABILITY FIX (TGT-160, found via scheduled hourly bug hunt
        JOB-003): D2TG::Config::is_transient_error never classified
        Telegram's own 429 ("Too Many Requests") rate-limit response as
        transient - only /timed out/i and /status 5\d\d/ matched.
        Telegram's Bot API documents 429 as a designed, expected,
        retryable rate-limit condition (a response body containing
        parameters.retry_after), not a genuine application error, but
        it was previously logged loudly as a genuine POLL ERROR on the
        monitored tira.policy.bridge stream every time routine rate
        limiting kicked in. Now returns true for a 429, matching the
        same treatment 5xx/timeout errors already get - run_once_safe's
        own retry/backoff timing is unchanged, only the loud-vs-silent
        logging decision changes. Deliberately does not read/honor
        Telegram's own parameters.retry_after value (would require
        parsing the response body, out of scope for this narrow
        classification fix).

