0.80  2026-09-08
      - FEATURE (TGT-103, user-supplied feature-gap analysis): the old
        ~/skills/tg blueprint had two dedicated senders for pushing a
        local file to Telegram as a photo or document message - this
        skill had no outbound-media primitive at all. New
        `D2TG::Telegram::send_photo`/`send_document` (mirroring
        `send_voice`'s own multipart pattern via a shared `_send_file`
        helper) plus a new `d2 tg.send [--bot <token>] <chat_id>
        <file_path> [--caption <text>] [--reply-to-message-id <id>]`
        entrypoint. Photo vs document is decided by file extension
        (`.jpg`/`.jpeg`/`.png`/`.gif`/`.webp` send as a photo, everything
        else as a document) - no content sniffing needed, Telegram
        accepts any file via `sendDocument` regardless. `chat_id` is
        validated numeric and the file's existence is checked before any
        network call, matching this skill's existing fail-fast
        conventions.

0.81  2026-09-08
      - FEATURE (TGT-111, user-supplied feature-gap analysis): the only
        way to know the poller is actually alive used to be reaching
        into Tira job metadata (`pid`/`last_output_at`) from outside
        this skill entirely. New `d2 tg.status [--db <alias> | -d
        <alias>]` reports the installed version and whether a live
        process currently holds the poller's own lock file, via a new
        `D2TG::Lock::is_held` - a pure, read-only liveness probe
        (`kill(0, $pid)`, no real signal) that deliberately never calls
        `D2TG::Lock::acquire`, since doing so to merely answer a status
        question could evict a genuinely live poller under this skill's
        own "last one wins" lock policy (TGT-084).

0.82  2026-09-08
      - FEATURE (TGT-116, live-experienced incident: a poller stayed
        alive and held its lock for 80+ minutes while doing nothing at
        all, silently losing a message - "alive" and "still genuinely
        cycling" turned out to be different questions): `d2 tg.poller`
        now writes a heartbeat after each bot/chat pair's own poll cycle
        - regardless of whether any message or error activity happened
        - via new `D2TG::Config::write_heartbeat`/`heartbeat_age`/
        `heartbeat_path` (mirroring `lock_path`'s own `.tira/`
        resolution, and writing atomically via a temp file + `rename`,
        so a concurrent `d2 tg.status` read or a crash mid-write can
        never observe a partial file). `d2 tg.status` (TGT-111) now also
        reports the heartbeat's age, flagging it `(STALE)` past 1200
        seconds - a threshold kept safely above the worst case a single
        pair's own poll cycle can legitimately take, including a slow
        voice transcription's full retry ladder (up to ~900s across
        `D2TG::Transcribe`'s medium->small->base tiers). Codex review
        caught this exact gap (a healthy, actively-transcribing poller
        could otherwise be falsely reported STALE against a tighter,
        once-per-full-cycle heartbeat), plus the non-atomic write. Full
        automatic restart-on-stale is intentionally NOT built here - a
        genuinely stuck poller cannot restart itself, so that needs
        either a second always-running watchdog process (recreating
        exactly the kind of supervisor this project's architecture
        deliberately avoids, Q-003) or a Tira-scheduled job doing the
        restarting - an operational/architecture decision, flagged as a
        follow-up.

0.83  2026-09-09
      - FEATURE (TGT-113, live-experienced incident: a poller crashed
        mid-version-bump race, never auto-restarted, and a SEPARATE
        orphaned instance under a different PID - with a stale command
        line missing '-d tira' - was found still running the entire
        time, competing for the same bot token's getUpdates queue):
        `d2 tg.poller` now warns on STDERR, immediately after acquiring
        its own lock, if it detects another live process whose command
        line looks like a poller instance - via new
        `D2TG::Lock::find_other_pollers`, a `/proc/<pid>/cmdline` scan
        independent of TGT-084's own lock-eviction (which only ever sees
        whichever single PID the lock FILE currently names). Report
        only, never a kill - a cmdline pattern match alone isn't strong
        enough evidence for this skill to act on unprompted. Codex
        review caught the first draft's pattern matching was an
        unanchored substring against a NUL-joined-as-spaces cmdline,
        false-positiving on names like `not-a-poller.pl`/
        `poller.pl.bak`/`--note=poller.pl` - fixed by matching each
        split argv element individually against an anchored
        `(?:^|/)poller\.pl$` pattern.

0.84  2026-09-09
      - FEATURE (TGT-104, user-supplied feature-gap analysis): a failed
        inbound photo/document download is now persisted to a retry
        queue instead of just printed and forgotten - the old
        `~/skills/tg` blueprint had this and the new skill dropped it,
        the exact loss mode that once cost a real receipt sent for
        safekeeping. New `D2TG::Store::record_failed_download`/
        `failed_downloads`/`remove_failed_download` back a new
        `failed_downloads` table, keyed uniquely by `(chat_id,
        message_id)` so Telegram's at-least-once delivery redelivering
        the same failed update refreshes the existing row instead of
        duplicating it. New `d2 tg.retry-download [<id> | --all]` lists
        and retries the queue via new `D2TG::Download::retry_failed_download`
        - a successful retry restores the message into `d2
        tg.history`/`d2 tg.unread` via `record_message`, not just
        deleting the queue row, before removing it. New
        `D2TG::Config::is_expired_file_error` distinguishes Telegram's
        own shape for a permanently-gone `file_id` ("file is no longer
        available"/"wrong file_id") from an ordinary, still-retryable
        failure, giving the former a distinguishable `RETRY EXPIRED`
        message. Two rounds of Codex review caught 5 real gaps before
        shipping (duplicate queue rows on redelivery, "file is
        temporarily unavailable" wrongly classified as permanent, an
        unwrapped queue-write that could crash the poller on a database
        error, retry success not restoring history, non-deterministic
        same-second ordering) - all fixed. A third, documentation-stage
        Codex review (backed by a web search of Telegram's own Bot API
        docs) then corrected a factual claim repeated across this
        ticket's docs: a `file_id` isn't documented as expiring on a
        short fixed clock - it's the one-hour-valid `file_path` a
        `getFile` call resolves it to that's short-lived, and every
        retry already calls `getFile` fresh. Docs reworded accordingly;
        no code change (the retry mechanism itself was always correct -
        it never assumed a `file_id` expiry window in the first place).

0.85  2026-09-09
      - FEATURE (TGT-105, user-supplied feature-gap analysis): a reply
        that went out text-only (TGT-083's own send-text-then-voice
        ordering means a late voice failure can leave one behind,
        always reported loudly at the time but easy to miss) is now
        flagged for after-the-fact discovery. New
        `D2TG::Store::record_sent_text`/`record_sent_voice`/
        `text_only_replies` back a new `sent_replies` table - a row
        whose `voice_message_id` is still NULL IS the text-only
        condition, no separate boolean to keep in sync.
        `D2TG::Reply::send_reply` records the text send immediately
        after it succeeds and the voice send once that also succeeds;
        `resend_voice` (TGT-109's own recovery path) clears the flag on
        a successful recovery. New `d2 tg.text-only-replies` lists any
        still-flagged reply, exit 1 if anything's flagged.
      - Codex review caught 2 HIGH-severity gaps before shipping: (1)
        the audit table wasn't bot-scoped, repeating the exact TGT-098
        cross-bot isolation gap for a Telegram group shared by more
        than one configured bot - fixed by adding `bot_key` to the
        table's own key and threading it through every call site
        including `cli/reply.pl --voice-only`'s recovery lookup; (2)
        `record_sent_voice` silently no-op'd when no matching row
        existed - fixed to warn loudly on STDERR instead. The
        inherent non-atomicity between a successful Telegram send and
        its store record is documented as an accepted, known
        limitation (mirroring TGT-104's own precedent) rather than
        solved with a larger durable-outbox design out of proportion
        with this ticket's scope. Also self-caught during the full
        test-suite run (not Codex): the first draft unconditionally
        assumed `send_message`'s return was an arrayref, breaking an
        existing test's own differently-shaped fake telegram - fixed
        to only attempt the extraction when a store is given, and to
        tolerate any other return shape via `eval`.

0.86  2026-09-09
      - FEATURE (TGT-114, user-supplied feature-gap analysis): `d2
        tg.reply` now refuses to send the exact same text to the same
        chat (and bot) twice within a short window (default 10s) -
        an accidentally re-run reply command, or a retry after a
        confirmed prior success whose voice half then failed, no
        longer delivers the same message a second time. New
        `D2TG::Store::is_recent_duplicate_reply` checks a new `text`
        column on the existing `sent_replies` table (TGT-105);
        `D2TG::Reply::send_reply` checks it before calling
        `send_message` at all, so a duplicate never reaches Telegram.
        Only active when a store is given.
      - Codex review caught a CRITICAL gap before shipping: the new
        `text` column needed the same `ALTER TABLE`-with-duplicate-
        tolerance migration pattern `messages.read_at` already uses - a
        bare `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` is a no-op against a database
        that already has `sent_replies` from an earlier TGT-105-only
        install. Also fixed: `window_seconds` now rejects negative/
        non-numeric values. Three further findings (no protection
        against an ambiguous `send_message` failure, no atomicity under
        genuine concurrency, second-granular timing) were confirmed
        real but accepted as documented limitations, matching this
        project's own established practice for this class of tradeoff.

